course: dictionary
lesson: 6257
title: Molecules: atoms joined up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Everything around you is made of tiny parts called atoms. Atoms rarely stay alone. They join together into groups, and a group of atoms joined together is called a molecule. «Atoms rarely stay alone: they join up into groups called molecules, a bit like Lego bricks clicking together.»
explain: A molecule is the smallest bit of a substance that still acts like that substance. «H2O» means two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom, and that is one molecule of water. The small 2 means there are two hydrogen atoms, and the 1 is left out because there is only one oxygen atom.
explain: Molecules are far too small to see, even with an ordinary microscope. You can only tell they are there by what they do. «Water molecules pull tightly on each other at the surface, making surface tension; pond skaters walk on it.»
fact: A molecule is made of two or more atoms joined together.
fact: A water molecule is H2O: two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.
fact: Molecules are much too small to see, even with an ordinary microscope.
fact: Geckos stick to glass because of very weak attractions between molecules, not because of glue.
fact: Heating makes molecules move faster.
exercise: What is a molecule? | *Atoms joined together in a group, the smallest bit that still acts like its substance | One single atom on its own | A large lump of any material
exercise: «H2O» means what? | Three atoms of carbon | One oxygen atom alone | Two oxygen atoms joined to one hydrogen atom | *Two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom
exercise: «Geckos stick to glass with millions of tiny toe hairs that cling by very weak attractions between molecules.» What helps the geckos cling? | Tiny magnets in their toes | Strong ropes made of hair | *Very weak attractions between molecules | Sticky glue from their feet
test: In which state are molecules packed tightly and vibrating? | *Solid | Liquid | Gas | Plasma
test: What does heating do to molecules? | Makes them stop moving | Makes them shrink | Makes them join into atoms | *Makes them move faster
exam: Can you see a single molecule with an ordinary microscope? | Yes, they look like tiny dots | Only in gases | Yes, if the microscope is very strong | *No, molecules are much too small to see
